---
export const title = 'Usage';
export const order = 100;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>wc-citation</code> links an answer to the record or page it is based on, so a person can check
  it. Facts come from the platform and words from the model; citations show which facts.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <p style='max-width: 36rem;'>
    Salary is readable by 4 people in Human resources<wc-citation
      label='1'
      value='role_4'
      headline='HR manager'
      supporting-text='Role · Human resources'
      href='#role-4'
    ></wc-citation>
    and by the Payroll integration<wc-citation
      label='2'
      value='rule_88'
      headline='Payroll integration reads Employee.Salary'
      supporting-text='Access rule · Human resources'
      href='#rule-88'
    >
      <span>Granted by Ada Lovelace on 3 October.</span>
    </wc-citation>. No other app can read it.
  </p>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Preview</h3>
<p>
  <code>headline</code> and <code>supporting-text</code> describe the source in a card that opens while
  the chip is hovered or focused. Anything in the default slot is added to the card. Without any of
  them there is no card, only the chip.
</p>

<h3>Links and buttons</h3>
<p>
  With <code>href</code> the chip is a link to the source. Without it, it is a button. Either way it
  fires <code>select</code> with the source's <code>value</code>; cancel the event to handle the chip
  yourself, for example to open the record in a side sheet.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <p>
    Fixed in 4.2.1<wc-citation id='citation-select' label='3' value='comment_17' headline="Ravi Kumar's comment"></wc-citation>.
  </p>
  <script is:inline>
    document.querySelector('#citation-select').addEventListener('select', function (event) {
      myConsole.log('select: ' + event.detail.value);
    });
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>In Markdown</h3>
<p>
  <code>wc-markdown</code> renders links written as <code>[2](cite:rule_88)</code> as citations, filled
  from its <code>citations</code> property. See the Markdown component's Citations section.
</p>

<h3>Accessibility</h3>
<p>
  The chip is named "Source 2: Payroll integration reads Employee.Salary", so it reads well out of
  context (the <code>citationLabel</code> string). The preview is a supplement: everything in it must
  also be reachable on the page the chip links to.
</p>
